<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Hamilton, 1st Viscount Claneboye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1560–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Hamilton, 1st Viscount Claneboye, was a Scottish schoolmaster turned agent of James VI who, with Hugh Montgomery, acquired much of Con O'Neill's lordship of Clandeboye in north Down in 1605. Their private settlement brought many Scottish colonists to Ulster before the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>, and he developed Bangor as his chief town.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hamilton was the eldest of six sons of Hans Hamilton, the first Protestant minister of Dunlop in East Ayrshire, and Jonet Denham. He was probably the James Hamilton who took a BA at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> in 1584 and an MA in 1585, and he taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>. About 1587 storms carried his ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, where he became master of the Free School in Ship Street with James Fullerton as usher; the young James Ussher was among their pupils. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> was founded in 1592, Hamilton and Fullerton became its first two Fellows, and Hamilton was bursar from 1598.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hamilton and Fullerton also reported to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> on Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>'s affairs in Ireland and sought Irish support for his succession. From August 1600 Hamilton was the king's resident agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and he carried the news of Elizabeth's death to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Fullerton later persuaded the king that the lands of Con O'Neill, which Hugh Montgomery had been promised for securing O'Neill's pardon, should be split three ways, with a third to Hamilton.</p><p>The first settlers from the Scottish Lowlands arrived in May 1606. Hamilton was knighted at Royston on 14 November 1609, bought Dufferin in 1610, and by 1611 Bangor had eighty houses. He sat for County Down in the parliament of 1613, repaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey">Bangor Abbey</a> church in 1617 and was created Viscount Claneboye on 4 May 1622. In 1637 he built the Custom House and Tower House at Bangor to rival Montgomery's port at Donaghadee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1641 he built a mausoleum for his parents at Dunlop with a school beside it, Clandeboye School; both still stand. When the Irish Rebellion of 1641 broke out he raised a regiment of 1,000 men, and with that of Hugh Montgomery, 2nd Viscount Montgomery, it spared their parts of County Down the worst of the damage. He died on 24 January 1644 and was buried at Bangor. His only son James, by his third wife Jane Phillips, became Earl of Clanbrassil in 1647; the title of Claneboye died out in 1675.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Hamilton, 1st Viscount Claneboye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Con O'Neill, the last lord, lost most of his lands to the Scots Hugh Montgomery and <b>James Hamilton</b> in 1605, opening north Down to large-scale Scottish settlement.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-clandeboye">Lordship of Clandeboye</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dunlop, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.7,-4.54&amp;year=1614" aria-label="Dunlop on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James Hamilton, 1st Viscount Claneboye"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4781 5360h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4781 5360h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.7,-4.54&amp;year=1614">What was here in 1614? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
